\begin{abstract}
This paper presents Categorical Hyperfunctors, a novel and mathematically rigorous framework for modeling and analyzing hyper-graphs through the lens of category theory. By representing nodes and hyper-edges as objects and morphisms, respectively, this approach facilitates precise interaction modeling via algebraic structures like posets and lattices. The framework employs Generalized Algebraic Data Types (GADTs) in a Haskell implementation, ensuring type safety and strict adherence to mathematical consistency.

Key innovations include lattice-theoretic operations such as supremum and infimum, leveraging partially ordered sets to rank entities and resolve hierarchical dependencies. Directed acyclic graphs (DAGs) derived from hyper-graphs are analyzed using topological sorting for applications such as task scheduling and dependency resolution. Practical applications demonstrate the versatility of this framework, including task scheduling where nodes represent tasks \& hyper-edges encode dependencies, and multi-modal reasoning where categorical limits aggregate constraints across modalities. By uniting advanced mathematical principles with computational efficiency, Categorical Hyperfunctors provide a robust paradigm for hyper-graph representation and real-world problem-solving.
\end{abstract}

