      $R(v_1, e) \leq R(v_2, e)$ along paths ensures a logical ranking aligned with hypergraph semantics.
    \item \textbf{Categorical Validity:}
      Rankings remain invariant under transformations and respect functorial behavior.
    \item \textbf{Operator-Based Insights:}
      Ranking highlights critical tasks and resources based on nuanced operator-defined semantics.
\end{itemize}

This integration creates a mathematically robust and computationally consistent framework for ranking entities in hypergraph-based systems, bridging theoretical rigor and practical applicability.

\section*{Large-Scale Hyper-Graphs}
Large-scale hypergraph frameworks require efficient data structures, parallelism, and algorithmic optimizations to handle their computational complexity. Sparse matrix representations, such as CSR or adjacency tensors, minimize memory usage while enabling fast access to vertices and hyperedges. Distributed computing frameworks, like Apache Spark's GraphX, allow parallel computation of relevance scores and dependency graphs across large datasets. Approximations, such as sampling and sketching, reduce computational overhead by estimating rankings without processing the entire graph. Moreover, GPU-accelerated libraries, like PyTorch Geometric, leverage modern hardware for fast computation of operator-specific relevance scores and dependency structures. These methods, combined with efficient topological sorting algorithms, ensure scalability, maintain accuracy, and preserve the mathematical properties of the ranking framework, such as operator invariance and categorical limits.


\section{Formal Analysis of Time and Space Complexity}

Let:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \( |V| \): Number of vertices in the hypergraph.
    \item \( |E| \): Number of hyperedges.
    \item \( |\Omega| \): Number of operators.
    \item \( d_{\text{avg}} \): Average number of vertices per hyperedge (hyperedge degree).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{1. Relevance Computation}

For each vertex-hyperedge pair \( (v, e) \), we compute \( R(v, e) = \sum_{o \in \Omega} w_o \cdot f_o(v, e) \).

\paragraph{Time Complexity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Per computation: \( O(|\Omega|) \), assuming \( f_o(v, e) \) is \( O(1) \).
    \item Total: \( O(|V| \cdot |E| \cdot |\Omega|) \).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Space Complexity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Storing the relevance scores: \( O(|V| \cdot |E|) \).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{2. Dependency Graph Construction}

Dependency graph edges \( E_d \) are created by comparing relevance scores \( R(v_1, e) \) and \( R(v_2, e) \) for all vertex pairs \( (v_1, v_2) \) over all hyperedges.

\paragraph{Time Complexity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Comparing \( (v_1, v_2) \) for each \( e \): \( O(d_{\text{avg}}^2) \).
    \item Total: \( O(|E| \cdot d_{\text{avg}}^2) \).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Space Complexity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Dependency edges: \( O(|V|^2) \) (worst case, fully connected dependency graph).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{3. Topological Sorting}

Topological sorting of the dependency graph \( D(H) = (V, E_d) \).

\paragraph{Time Complexity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Using Kahn's Algorithm or DFS-based sorting: \( O(|V| + |E_d|) \).
    \item Worst-case \( |E_d| \): \( O(|V|^2) \), so \( O(|V| + |V|^2) = O(|V|^2) \).
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Space Complexity}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Storing the dependency graph: \( O(|E_d|) = O(|V|^2) \).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{4. Total Complexity}

\paragraph{Time Complexity}
The overall time complexity combines all steps:
\[
O(|V| \cdot |E| \cdot |\Omega| + |E| \cdot d_{\text{avg}}^2 + |V|^2)
\]
\begin{itemize}
    \item Dominated by \( O(|V| \cdot |E| \cdot |\Omega|) \) for relevance computation and \( O(|E| \cdot d_{\text{avg}}^2) \) for dependency graph construction.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Space Complexity}
The total space complexity:
\[
O(|V| \cdot |E| + |V|^2)
\]
\begin{itemize}
    \item Dominated by \( O(|V| \cdot |E|) \) for relevance scores and \( O(|V|^2) \) for dependency graph storage.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{5. Optimization Insights}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Sparse Graphs:} For sparse hypergraphs (\( |E|, d_{\text{avg}} \ll |V| \)), the dependency graph \( |E_d| \) is much smaller, reducing \( O(|V|^2) \) to a linear factor \( O(|E| \cdot d_{\text{avg}}) \).
    \item \textbf{Distributed Computing:} Partitioning \( V \) and \( E \) across clusters reduces per-node storage and computational load.
    \item \textbf{Approximation:} Techniques like sampling and sketching lower \( d_{\text{avg}} \), reducing \( O(d_{\text{avg}}^2) \) dependency graph costs.
\end{enumerate}

By leveraging these insights, the framework can efficiently handle even large-scale hypergraphs.


\section{Application in Communication Networks or Network Systems - Scheduling and Dependency Resolution in 5G Network Slicing}

\subsection*{Use Case in Communication Networks or Networked Systems}
A practical use case of \textbf{Categorical Hyperfunctors} can be demonstrated in \textbf{multi-agent coordination in communication networks}, where tasks and dependencies are represented as hyper-graphs.

\subsubsection*{\textbf{Scenario}: Scheduling and Dependency Resolution in 6G Network Slicing}

\section*{Network Slicing and Orchestration in 6G Networks}

\subsection*{The Problem}
In 6G networks, \textbf{network slicing} allows the creation of multiple virtualized and isolated network "slices," each tailored to meet specific application requirements (e.g., ultra-low latency, massive IoT connectivity, high throughput). \textbf{Orchestration} involves efficiently allocating resources to these slices while ensuring quality of service (QoS), minimizing cost, and maximizing performance.

The problem is challenging due to:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dynamic and heterogeneous requirements:} Different slices demand varying bandwidth, latency, and reliability.
    \item \textbf{Limited physical resources:} Computational, storage, and network resources must be optimally shared.
    \item \textbf{End-to-end QoS guarantees:} Meeting SLA (Service Level Agreement) constraints for all slices across the network.
    \item \textbf{Multi-domain complexity:} Slices span multiple domains (e.g., radio access networks, core networks, and edge computing nodes), making coordination and orchestration more difficult.
\end{itemize}


