We denote with $n_s$ the number of labeled samples per class. For the \RING and \BLOB datasets, the experiments are performed with one labeled sample per class, $n_s=1$ (i.e., 10 labeled samples which is a portion of 0.001 labeled data in the training dataset). For the experiments on the \MNIST dataset, the primary analysis is performed using $n_s=10$ labeled samples per class (i.e., 100~labeled samples which is 0.00167 labeled data in the training dataset). A sensitivity analysis is performed on the number of labeled data for MNIST (\textbf{RQ3}), the most challenging dataset, to see the impact on the results. Thus, additional experiments are performed with $n_s=60$ and $n_s=100$ labeled samples per class.




