\section{Introduction}

% Structural results for quantum states are crucial to understanding the difficulties of preparing such states, examples include entanglement area law for 1D gapped hams, which lead to efficient RG, CMI decay leads to efficient prep of thermal states, and recent high temp Gibbs state results
Studying quantum many-body systems through computational means has been a central topic in computational physics, chemistry, and quantum computing. Mathematically rigorous results that guarantee the efficient computation of ground state and Gibbs state properties typically rely on structural knowledge of these states. 
For example, the ground state of a 1D gapped local Hamiltonian can be efficiently obtained, as the area-law entanglement scaling ensures that it admits a matrix product state representation \cite{landau2015polynomial,hastings2007area}. Moreover, Gibbs states can be efficiently prepared on quantum computers when they exhibit exponential decay of correlations and satisfy  certain Markov property \cite{BrandaoKastoryano2019finite,chen2025quantum}.
