Moreover, recall that by definition $S_{i-1}$ has just one more element than $S_i$. 
Then, the local $G_b$ in $H^{S_{i-1}}-H^{S_i}$ can be characterized by the following set
    \[T_2 := \{b : v \in \supp(G_b) \text{ for } \{v\} = S_{i-1} - S_{i} \text{ and } G_b \in H^{S_{i-1}}\}.\]
    % \YT{$G_b$ above also needs to be contained in $H^{S_{i-1}}$?}
We have therefore obtained a decomposition
    \begin{equation} \label{eq:inductive_c}p_{t+1}(H^{S_{i-1}}, H^{S_i}) =\frac{\beta}{2(t+1)}\left( \sum_{ a \in T_{1, j}, j} (2\lambda_a c_{j,t})(G_a\Gamma_{j,t}) + \sum_{j, a \in T_2} (\lambda_a c_{j,t}) (-\Gamma_{j,t} G_a)\right).
    \end{equation}
Further note that by induction, every Majorana string in Eq.~(\ref{eq:inductive_c}) is a $(t+1)$-fold product of terms $G_a$ and is thus at most $\mathcal{R}(t+1)$-local. Moreover, note that $\supp(\Gamma_{j,t})\subseteq S_{i-1}$ since both $H^{S_i}$ and $H^{S_{i-1}}$ are supported on $S_{i-1}.$ 

