\section{Conclusion}

Motivated by recent progress on the lack of entanglement in high-temperature Gibbs states of quantum spin systems \cite{bakshi2024high}, in this work we showed that high-temperature Gibbs states of fermionic systems are probabilistic mixtures of fermionic Gaussian states. Fermionic Gaussian states are completely determined by their correlation matrices, which enables efficient classical representation of these states. Based on this structural result, we then proposed an efficient algorithm to sample from this distribution of fermionic Gaussian states, thus enabling efficient computation of observable expectation values and sampling from the Gibbs states.

Besides techniques developed in \cite{bakshi2024high}, our results rely on a new adaptive pairing procedure for Majorana modes. While for quantum spin systems locality of the operators can imply commutativity, such connection is only possible in the fermionic case with an appropriate pairing.

Our result sharpens the the boundary between quantum advantage and classical simulability for fermionic systems. Recent results on the mixing time of weakly interacting fermionic systems shows that at any constant temperature, the corresponding Gibbs states can be efficiently prepared on a quantum computer \cite{TongZhan2024fast,SmidMeisterBertaBondesan2025polynomial}. Our result shows that in the high-temperature regime, this mixing time result does not lead to a super-polynomial quantum advantage. However, for a temperature below the threshold considered in this work, the existence of significant quantum advantage remains an open problem.
Given the importance of the structural result in the design of the algorithm, it is natural to ask whether weakly interacting fermionic Gibbs states can also be expressed as mixtures of fermionic Gaussian states.

The connection between fermionic Gaussian mixtures and simulability was also explored in the simulation of fermionic circuits \cite{DeMelo2013power}. Given the prominent role played by this structure in the simulability of fermionic systems, it is of interest to explore ways to test whether a fermionic quantum state is a mixture of Gaussian states or not. This question can be asked in at least two different settings: Firstly, given a quantum state that is the ground state or the Gibbs state of a non-quadratic fermionic Hamiltonian, can we distinguish whether it is a mixture of fermionic Gaussian states? Secondly, if we have an unknown quantum state on an experimental fermionic system, what are the sample and computational complexities of answering such a question?
Answering these questions can help us better understand the classical simulability of fermionic systems and the boundary of quantum advantage.

