\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Pulmonary segments are anatomically and functionally independent subdivisions of pulmonary lobes without explicit boundaries~(Fig.~\ref{fig:lobe_segment}). In lung anatomy, each segment includes its corresponding bronchus, artery, and vein, establishing their boundaries along intersegmental veins~\citep{Frick2017Segmentectomies, Oizumi2014TechniquesTD}. By definition, there are ten segments in the right lung and eight to ten in the left lung, depending on individual variations~\citep{Boyden1945IntrahilarRS, Jackson1943CorrelatedAA, Ugalde2007LobesFB}. The reconstruction of pulmonary segments is crucial in clinical practice, as it assists in the localization of lung diseases and planning surgical interventions, such as segmentectomy, a type of surgical intervention for non-small-cell lung cancer due to its capability to preserve greater pulmonary function~\citep{Saji2022Jcog0802, Schuchert2007AnatomicST,Wisnivesky2010LimitedRT,Handa2021PostoperativePF,Harada2005FunctionalAR,Saji2022Jcog0802}. It would lower operation time and blood loss, resulting in lower recurrence rates and better survival outcomes. 
%In recent years, an increasing number of studies have demonstrated that segmentectomy is supported as the standard of care for small, peripheral non–small-cell lung cancer due to its capability to preserve greater pulmonary function, lower operation time and blood loss, and achieve comparable recurrence rates and survival outcomes~\citep{Saji2022Jcog0802, Wisnivesky2010LimitedRT, Schuchert2007AnatomicST, Handa2021PostoperativePF, Harada2005FunctionalAR}.
%However, all of these efforts rely on the reliable reconstruction of pulmonary segments. For instance, accurate surgical margins are required to measure the recurrence rates of segmentectomy~\citep{Schuchert2007AnatomicST, Oizumi2011AnatomicTP} 
