\section{Methodology}

%The primary challenge of deep learning in automatic pulmonary segment reconstruction is the dilemma between memory efficiency and high-resolution output. Traditional CNN-based methods compromise the resolution for a coarse output, which is insufficient for pulmonary segment segmentation. 
Reconstructing pulmonary segments using conventional voxel-to-voxel dense prediction-based methods poses challenges due to the varying sizes of CT images. First, they demand substantial memory resources, especially when applied to high-resolution 3D volumes, thereby limiting their utility in high-resolution data scenarios. Second, when operating at \revised{reduced} resolutions, these methods yield coarse segmentation, which is inadequate for this particular task. In contrast, implicit functions represent continuous iso-surfaces of shapes, and generate outputs at arbitrary resolutions, even with low-resolution inputs.
As a solution, we propose an implicit function-based approach that begins with a pre-trained template network and employs two implicit functions to transform and correct the \revised{fixed pre-trained} template, ultimately achieving the desired reconstruction.

\subsection{Preliminaries: Neural Implicit Function}
The implicit function is typically represented as a signed distance function (SDF) or occupancy function given a query voxel grid coordinate $\textbf{p}$ as input. While SDF denotes the signed distance between a given coordinate and the nearest point on the surface of the 3D shape $S$, the occupancy function ${F}$ maps the input 3D coordinates usually to an occupancy output $z \in [0,1]$ as the probability of the point belonging to a specific class or a series of features associated with the location. Let $n$ be the output classes, the mapping can be formulated as:

\begin{equation}
{F}(\textbf{p}) = z : \mathbb(R)^3 \rightarrow \mathbb(R)^n
\end{equation}

\subsection{Architecture Overview}

As a start of our pipeline (Fig. \ref{fig:pipeline}), a template network ${T}$ maps a pre-trained encoding vector $\textbf{v}$ into a template of the pulmonary segment segmentation $\textbf{t}$, representing the mean shape of the target dataset, \revised{illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:template}.} \revised{The generation process can be} formulated as: 
\begin{equation}
T(\textbf{v}) = \textbf{t} 
\label{equ:template}
\end{equation}

Simultaneously at the image input, the architecture takes a 3D-volume $\textbf{X}$, which can be a CT image, a binary volume of pulmonary structures (i.e., pulmonary bronchi, artery, and vein), or their combinations. Taking the volumetric input, a CNN\revised{-based} encoder $f$ extracts multi-scale feature pyramids $f(\textbf{X}) = \{\emph{F}_{1}, \emph{F}_{2}, ..., \emph{F}_{n}\}$. For a query point $\textbf{p}$, the corresponding multi-scale feature around point $\textbf{p}$ can be acquired through tri-linear interpolation from the feature pyramids, written as $\{\emph{F}_{1}(\textbf{p}), \emph{F}_{2}(\textbf{p}), ..., \emph{F}_{n}(\textbf{p})\}$. Subsequently, the multi-scale point feature is concatenated with the query coordinates of $\textbf{p}$ to form a point encoding $\emph{F}(\textbf{p})$ as the input to a Deformation Network  ${D}$ to predict a deformation field as:
\begin{equation}
D(f(\textbf{X}), \textbf{p}) = \Delta d : \mathbb(R)^c \times \mathbb(R)^3 \rightarrow \mathbb(R)^3
\label{equ:deformation}
\end{equation}
%\begin{equation}
%C(s, p) = \Delta c : \mathbb(R)^c \times \mathbb(R)^3 \rightarrow \mathbb(R)^{19}
%\label{equ:correction}
%\end{equation}
With the deformation field generated by $D$, the original 3D-volume input is transformed to a desired shape by addition to align with the interpolated template. This process yields a raw occupancy field that retains the initial occupancy information.

%Given the formulations presented in Equation \ref{equ:template} and Equation \ref{equ:imp_func}, the formulation of our pipeline can be expressed as follows:
%\begin{equation}
%F(s, p, t) = F(p + D(s, p), T(v)) 
%\end{equation}

Next, a Correction Network $C$ is incorporated to alleviate the drawback associated with template-based prediction, especially in cases where the input shape deviates significantly from the template. Similar to the Deformation Network $D$, the Correction Network $C$ is also an implicit function that consumes the point encoding $\emph{F}(\textbf{p})$ and produces a correction field, 
\begin{equation}
C(f(\textbf{X}), \textbf{p}) = \Delta c : \mathbb(R)^c \times \mathbb(R)^3 \rightarrow \mathbb(R)^{19}
\label{equ:correction}
\end{equation} 
The correction field directly corrects the raw occupancy field, yielding the final occupancy prediction.

Thus, given a 3D image input $\textbf{X}$, a query point $\textbf{p}$ within the image foreground, and the pre-trained template vector $\textbf{v}$, the proposed \impulsep{ImPulSe+} pipeline, $\textit{H}$, for pulmonary segment segmentation can be formulated as:
\begin{equation}
\textit{H}(\textbf{X}, \textbf{p}, \textbf{v}) = T(\textbf{v})(\textbf{p} + D(f(\textbf{X}), \textbf{p})) + C(f(\textbf{X}), \textbf{p})
\end{equation}


