Nevertheles, achieving satisfactory reconstruction results using only pulmonary shapes without image data is still possible (\ie{}, \textit{LBAV}, ). Therefore, in Sec.~\ref{sec:shape_based_ablation}, we will analyze shape-\revised{only} reconstruction for pulmonary segments.

\section{Shape-based Reconstruction: From \revised{Shapes} to Segments}
\label{sec:shape_based_ablation}
\revised{In our experiment setup, shape-based reconstruction refers to using binary shapes~(Sec.~\ref{subsec:dataset_overview}) as input for pulmonary segments generation.} The shapes for pulmonary tree-like structures such as pulmonary bronchi and vessels can be obtained from CT images. \revised{Due to privacy concern, raw CT images will not be publicly available. Therefore, the model performance on shape-based input will serve as a benchmark for future comparison for the pulmonary segment reconstruction task.} As previously \revised{explained} in Sec.~\ref{sec:introduction} \revised{and Sec.~\ref{subsec:ps_reconstruction}}, the semantic \revised{multi-class} reconstruction of pulmonary segments, \revised{as subdivisions of pulmonary lobes~(Fig.~\ref{fig:lobe_segment})},  does not involve explicit boundary definition but rather is defined by the associated tree structures of a specific segment \revised{class}. \revised{Therefore, we believe that pulmonary tree-structure shapes contain enough information to achieve anatomically correct pulmonary segment reconstruction.} 

\subsection{Experiments Setting}
We claim that the reconstruction of pulmonary segments could be achieved with only shape-based inputs, using pulmonary bronchi, arteries, veins \revised{as tree structures}\revised{~(Fig.~\ref{fig:dataset}: 3rd \& 4th column)} and 5 lobes\revised{~(Fig.~\ref{fig:lobe_segment})} \revised{as overall shape contour}. 

\revised{In the shape-based experiments, we first perform evaluation on the proposed \impulsep{ImPulSe+} with pulmonary lobe~(Tab.~\ref{tab:input_ablation}, {\it L}) as 1-channel input as initial division of 5 lobes, without the tree-shaped structures' anatomical guidance. Then, we additionally gather the 3 tree structures, to form a 4-channel binary volume~(Tab.~\ref{tab:input_ablation}, {\it LBAV}) to evaluate the performance of the proposed network with shape-based inputs. The two experiments should comparatively indicate the contribution of pulmonary tree shapes in this task. Additionally, the same comparative experiments are conducted on generated shapes predicted by nnU-Net on raw images. This evaluation attests the practicality of the proposed method by assuming that the ground truth shapes are unavailable. Finally, an ablation study on architecture, similar to Sec.~\ref{subsubsec:arch_Abl}, is conducted on the shape-based 4-channel input.}


