\section{Can LLM Agents Exhibit Prosocial Behavior?}\label{section3}

This section evaluates whether LLM agents can express context-sensitive prosocial behavior in a manner comparable to humans. We first assess their baseline prosocial intentions across diverse scenarios, then investigate how individual psychological traits shape these behaviors. Finally, we examine whether LLMs adapt their responses under explicit prosocial policy interventions.

\subsection{Baseline Prosocial Intention in Diverse Scenarios}

We begin by assessing the baseline prosocial tendencies of LLM agents across six representative scenarios. Each agent receives a neutral prompt and rates its willingness to perform a prosocial act on a 7-point Likert scale. Figure~\ref{fig:beeswarm} (left) shows the distribution of prosocial intentions across scenarios. GPT-3.5 exhibits the highest overall prosociality, particularly in tasks involving helping, donating, and cooperation. GPT-4o and DeepSeek-v3 display relative better alignment with human responses across multiple contexts. Qwen-2.5 shows more polarized behavior, responding positively in low-cost or public-good scenarios, but less so in interpersonal settings such as sharing. LLaMA-3 consistently produces the most conservative ratings across all conditions. We also observe that LLMs tend to be more prosocial in scenarios with high normative expectations and less prosocial when the cost of action is high, suggesting a sensitivity to structural dimensions of prosocial tasks. Figure~\ref{fig:beeswarm} (right) summarizes average prosociality across all tasks. GPT-3.5 ranks highest overall, followed by human participants, with GPT-4o and Qwen-2.5 at comparable levels. DeepSeek-v3 and LLaMA-3 fall lower on the scale. These results suggest that advanced LLMs can express prosocial intentions that approach human-level patterns, although their responses remain sensitive to scenario context and model-specific characteristics. This baseline evaluation sets the stage for further analysis of individual traits and institutional influences on prosocial behavior.


