\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
This work presents \textsc{ProSim}, a modular framework for simulating prosocial behavior in LLM agents. Through three progressive studies, we demonstrate that LLMs can exhibit context-sensitive prosociality, respond to fairness norms, and adjust their behavior under policy cues—showing alignment with key aspects of human social cognition. Beyond individual behavior, our simulations reveal a broader social phenomenon: structural inequities, such as reward or burden asymmetry, not only suppress prosocial motivation at the agent level but also propagate through social networks, leading to collective norm erosion. These findings highlight the potential of LLM-based simulations as tools for uncovering social dynamics and evaluating institutional impacts.





