\section{Related Work}

\textbf{LLMs as Social Simulation Agents.}  
Recent advances in LLMs have positioned them as powerful computational proxies for human social behavior, supporting simulations across domains such as social psychology~\cite{xu2024ai}, behavioral economics~\cite{horton2023large}, and multi-agent systems~\cite{guo2024large}. These applications typically span three levels of analysis: individual, interactional, and societal.

At the individual level, LLMs are used to simulate cognitive processes in psychological assessments~\cite{karra2022estimating}, decision-making tasks~\cite{horton2023large}, and human-computer interaction experiments~\cite{farn2023tooltalk,chalamalasetti2023clembench}, helping to assess the cognitive plausibility of model behavior. Recent work has also highlighted their potential for metacognitive capabilities in adaptive information retrieval~\cite{zhou2024metacognitive}, further enhancing their utility in modeling human-like reasoning.  At the interactional level, LLM agents participate in cooperative and competitive settings, enabling studies of emergent behaviors such as group coordination~\cite{xiong2023examining,qian2023chatdev,baker2024simulating}, moral judgment~\cite{hamilton2023blind,he2024simucourt}, and strategic thinking~\cite{light2023text,wu2023deciphering}.  At the societal level, researchers leverage LLMs to simulate complex social dynamics, including norm emergence~\cite{li2024culturepark}, collective action~\cite{chuang2023wisdom,zhu2024generative}, political polarization~\cite{wang2024decoding}, and large-scale opinion diffusion within networked populations~\cite{chuang2023simulating,liu2024skepticism}.  

Despite these advances, a critical open question remains: can LLM agents demonstrate stable and context-sensitive prosocial behavior? Addressing this question is essential for understanding the limits and capabilities of LLM-based social simulation.


\textbf{Prosocial Behavior Dynamics.}
Prosocial behaviors are voluntary actions intended to benefit others and underpin social cohesion, cooperation, and institutional trust \cite{grueneisen2022development}. Extensive research has established that prosocial behaviors are influenced by individual psychological traits \cite{carlo2005interplay,thielmann2020personality,thielmann2022dispositional}, contextual conditions \cite{simpson2015beyond,labroo2023reconsidering}, and social structures \cite{baxter2022local,andreoni2021higher}.

Key psychological factors influencing prosociality include empathic concern \cite{cameron2022empathy,wu2024motive}, moral identity \cite{vcehajic2024threaten,yang2025relationship}, altruistic predispositions \cite{amitha2024altruistic}, and perceived social responsibility \cite{pastor2024study,alfirevic2023role}. Individuals tend to exhibit stronger prosocial intentions when they perceive their actions as aligned with social norms \cite{graf2023social,rudert2022following}, fair treatment \cite{caserta2023good,tu2022treat}, or group identity \cite{wang2021socioeconomic,xia2021religious}. Importantly, prosociality is highly sensitive to situational cues and network interactions \cite{soutschek2017dopaminergic,elbaek2023subjective}. Empirical findings indicate that perceptions of unfairness and exposure to antisocial norms can significantly undermine cooperative behaviors \cite{yang2025relationship,mekvabishvili2023prosocial,silva2022integrating}, highlighting the importance of modeling prosocial behavior as dynamically evolving within structured social contexts. Building upon this literature, our work aims to systematically explore whether and how LLM agents exhibit comparable patterns of prosocial responsiveness within an integrated simulation framework.

\section{Limitations and Future Work}\label{appendix:limitations}

While this work provides a structured and multi-level evaluation of prosocial behavior in LLM agents, several areas present opportunities for future investigation:

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=1pt, topsep=2pt, parsep=1pt]

\item \textbf{Scope of Scenarios.} Our evaluation focuses on a curated set of prosocial tasks and policy interventions designed for controlled comparison. Although these tasks span diverse behavioral categories, future work may explore additional domains such as strategic cooperation, long-term interaction, or emotionally charged decision-making.

\item \textbf{Interpretability and Cognitive Fidelity.} Our analysis emphasizes behavioral outcomes and integrates trait-based modeling and SHAP interpretability. However, further research is needed to probe whether observed behaviors stem from internalized social reasoning or reflect surface-level generalization from training data.

\item \textbf{Human Alignment Benchmarking.} Our human-agent comparisons rely on a controlled experimental setup with limited participant diversity and single-turn tasks. Extending to more interactive, longitudinal, and ecologically valid human-agent interactions would allow for deeper assessment of social alignment.
\end{itemize}

These limitations point to valuable directions for future research. Advancing the interpretability of agent behavior, incorporating learning and memory across interactions, and simulating more complex multi-agent environments will be critical for building socially aligned and cognitively plausible LLM agents. Additionally, combining LLM-based simulations with human-in-the-loop experiments could enhance the ecological validity of findings and support the use of artificial agents as scalable tools for social scientific discovery.


\section{Implementation Details}
\label{ap:details}
\subsection{Agent Profile Design}\label{appendix:agent_profiles}

To generate heterogeneous agents grounded in empirically plausible profiles, we construct a synthetic population based on national demographic statistics and meta-analytic trait distributions. This setup enables realistic variation in both sociodemographic and psychological dimensions.

\subsubsection{Demographic Attributes}

Each agent is initialized with a set of demographic attributes sampled from population-level distributions reported by the National Bureau of Statistics of China.\footnote{\url{https://www.stats.gov.cn/english/}}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=1pt, topsep=2pt, parsep=1pt]

\item \textbf{Gender.} Agents are assigned male or female labels with probabilities of 51.1\% and 48.9\%, respectively, reflecting the national gender ratio.

\item \textbf{Age.} An agent’s age is determined in two steps. First, an age bracket is selected according to census proportions. Then, an integer age is drawn uniformly within the selected bracket (e.g., if the bracket is 20–24, a value is sampled from [20, 24]).

\item \textbf{Marital Status.} Agents are labeled as single (19.9\%), married (71.2\%), divorced (2.7\%), or widowed (6.3\%) based on national statistics.

\item \textbf{Education.} Educational attainment is sampled from five levels: No Formal Education (5.7\%), Primary (17.6\%), Junior High (29.5\%), Senior High (23.0\%), and College or Above (24.2\%).

\item \textbf{Monthly Income.} Income is sampled uniformly from five predefined brackets representing socioeconomic strata. Each bracket has equal probability (20\%) and corresponds to specific income levels (in RMB): ¥9{,}215, ¥20{,}442, ¥32{,}195, ¥50{,}220, and ¥95{,}055.

\item \textbf{Occupation.} Occupational labels are age-dependent. Agents under 21 are categorized as “Student,” and those over 60 as “Retired.” For agents aged 21–60, employment status is sampled probabilistically: 5.2\% are assigned “Unemployed,” and the remaining 94.8\% are allocated one of 18 occupation types (e.g., Worker, Doctor, Civil Servant) according to reported occupational distributions.
\end{itemize}



\subsubsection{Psychological Traits}

Each agent is also characterized by a suite of psychological variables, partitioned into \textit{prosocial dispositions} and the \textit{Big Five} personality traits. All scores are drawn from truncated Gaussian distributions to reflect empirical variability while ensuring bounded and interpretable results within the interval $[1, 5]$.

\paragraph{Prosocial Dispositions.} The following four variables are used to represent prosocial orientation:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=1pt, topsep=2pt, parsep=1pt]
  \item \textbf{Empathic Concern}
  \item \textbf{Moral Identity}
  \item \textbf{Social Responsibility}
  \item \textbf{Altruistic Tendency}
\end{itemize}
These are sampled from a truncated normal distribution $\mathcal{N}(\mu=3,\ \sigma=0.5)$, bounded to the interval $[1, 5]$.

\paragraph{Big Five Personality Traits.}Based on meta-analytic norms, each of the five personality dimensions is sampled from a trait-specific truncated normal distribution as follows:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, itemsep=1pt, topsep=2pt, parsep=1pt]
  \item \textbf{Agreeableness}: $\mathcal{N}(\mu=3.69,\ \sigma=0.47)$
  \item \textbf{Conscientiousness}: $\mathcal{N}(\mu=3.29,\ \sigma=0.59)$
  \item \textbf{Neuroticism}: $\mathcal{N}(\mu=2.96,\ \sigma=0.67)$
  \item \textbf{Openness}: $\mathcal{N}(\mu=3.57,\ \sigma=0.59)$
  \item \textbf{Extraversion}: $\mathcal{N}(\mu=3.19,\ \sigma=0.66)$
\end{itemize}
All scores are truncated to lie within the range $[1, 5]$ to ensure internal consistency across traits.

