\subsection{BDI Analysis of Prosocial Policy Interventions}

We focused on GPT-3.5 to examine the effects of four prosocial policy interventions on individual behavior. These interventions included Economic Incentives, Moral Indoctrination, Regulatory Enforcement, and Social Comparison. Using Individual3 from Table\ref{tab:individual_information} as a representative case, we illustrate the prosocial tendencies and underlying reasoning processes after each policy intervention.

The following example illustrates the reasoning process in a donation scenario, where \blueText{blue} text indicates the positive function of the policy and \redText{red} text indicates the negative function of the policy.


\begin{policybox}{Economic Incentives}{4}
\policycontent{
  Donating can make a positive impact, especially for children’s education. \blueText{The tax deduction and matching government funds are good incentives}, but donating still means giving up some of my own money.
}{
  I desire to help, but I also want to make sure I’m financially secure and responsible.
}{
  I might be willing to donate, especially if \blueText{the matching program increases my impact}. However, I would need to weigh it against my current financial situation.
}
\end{policybox}

\begin{policybox}{Moral Indoctrination}{4}
\policycontent{
  \blueText{I believe donating to charity is a good thing to do, especially when it’s for a meaningful cause like supporting children’s education.} However, I’m also mindful of my own financial situation.
}{
  I would desire to donate, but I might hesitate to give up some of my own money, especially if I feel I’m already managing my own budget tightly. The desire to help is there, but it has to be balanced with my own financial needs.
}{
  I would likely donate, but it would depend on the amount and my current financial flexibility. A smaller donation might be more feasible, so it depends on how I feel at the time.
}
\end{policybox}

\begin{policybox}{Regulatory Enforcement}{5}
\policycontent{
  I believe that supporting children’s education is an important cause, and charity donations help make a positive impact. \redText{However, the requirement to donate feels somewhat intrusive.}
}{
  I desire to contribute to meaningful causes, but I would \redText{prefer to choose where my money goes and when to donate}. \redText{The mandatory aspect makes it less appealing}.
}{
  \blueText{Since the donation is required and there are penalties for non-compliance, I would likely donate.} The amount isn’t too burdensome, \redText{but I would prefer a more voluntary approach}.
}
\end{policybox}

\begin{policybox}{Social Comparison}{4}
\policycontent{
  I believe in supporting charitable causes, especially those that benefit underprivileged children, \redText{but donating to public causes can sometimes feel like it’s more about public recognition than actually helping}.
}{red
  While I do feel a desire to help children, \redText{the public pressure to donate, especially when seeing others' names on a leaderboard, might drive me to feel like I *should* contribute}, even if I’m not personally as motivated by the cause itself.
}{
  While I might donate to \blueText{avoid the feeling of being left out}, I would likely do so out of a sense of social obligation more than a strong desire to support the cause itself.
}
\end{policybox}


