\section{Related Work}
\label{sec.relate_works}
\subsection{Point-Point registration}

Point-Point registration aims to estimate the rigid transformation between two point clouds by aligning their geometric structures. Traditional approaches, such as the Iterative Closest Point algorithm~\cite{besl1992method}, iteratively compute nearest neighbor correspondences and solve for the transformation using least-squares optimization~\cite{arun1987least}. While effective under small initial misalignments, these approaches are highly sensitive to noise, outliers, and poor initialization, often resulting in convergence to local minima. To address these limitations, recent research has explored learning-based registration frameworks that leverage neural networks to extract robust point-wise features and establish accurate correspondences. For example, FCGF~\cite{choy2019fully} proposes a 3D fully convolutional network based on sparse convolutions~\cite{graham20183d} to generate high-quality features for matching. D3Feat~\cite{bai2020d3feat} introduces a unified architecture that jointly detects keypoints and learns their descriptors, using KPConv~\cite{thomas2019kpconv} to better capture local geometric structures.

Beyond keypoint-based strategies, recent advances have demonstrated the effectiveness of keypoint-free and RANSAC-free pipelines. These approaches often adopt a coarse-to-fine scheme to first estimate a global transformation, followed by local refinement. Approaches such as Predator~\cite{huang2021predator} and GeoTransformer~\cite{qin2022geometric} bypass traditional sampling and matching paradigms by leveraging attention mechanisms and global context, significantly improving robustness to partial overlaps and challenging initial poses. Despite the success of these approaches in intra-modal 3D registration, they often assume similar sensing modalities and dense geometry. In contrast, cross-modal scenarios (such as registering a sparse LiDAR point cloud to an RGB image) pose additional challenges due to differences in density, noise characteristics, and the lack of shared appearance cues.


\subsection{Pixel-Pixel Registration}

Pixel-Pixel registration aims to align images captured from different viewpoints or at different times by establishing correspondences directly between pixels \cite{han2024generalized, zhou2023e3cm}. Accurate registration underpins various applications, such as stereo camera extrinsic calibration \cite{zhao2024dive} and 3D reconstruction \cite{fan2018road}. Traditional registration pipelines often rely on handcrafted features such as SIFT~\cite{lowe2004distinctive} and ORB~\cite{rublee2011orb} to detect and match keypoints across images. However, these approaches often struggle under significant appearance variations, illumination changes, or low-texture scenes.

With the advent of deep learning, learning-based approaches have significantly advanced the state of the art in image registration by jointly learning robust feature detectors and descriptors. 
Detector-free approaches, such as LoFTR~\cite{sun2021loftr}, address this issue by directly predicting dense correspondences from full-image features. LoFTR employs a coarse-to-fine transformer architecture to capture both global context and fine details, yielding robust registration performance even in the presence of weak textures or large viewpoint differences. Once reliable correspondences are established, classical geometric approaches such as the PnP algorithm~\cite{lepetit2009ep} or Bundle Adjustment~\cite{triggs1999bundle} can be applied to estimate the relative camera pose, recovering rotation and translation parameters. These optimization-based approaches remain standard for downstream applications like simultaneous localization and mapping and 3D reconstruction.

In summary, modern image registration has greatly benefited from learning-based strategy, enabling more accurate and reliable alignment across diverse settings. However, most existing works primarily focus on intra-modal cases, while registration across different sensing modalities, such as LiDAR and camera, remains a challenging and underexplored problem.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
    \centering
