\subsection{Calibration Accuracy}
\subsubsection{Sparse Point Cloud}
Table~\ref{tab.cmp_kitti00} presents quantitative comparisons between our method and a series of state-of-the-art target-free LiDAR Camera Extrinsic Calibration (LCEC) approaches on the KITTI Odometry sequence 00. The upper part of the table includes methods that directly regress poses from image pairs, while the lower part lists methods that first perform cross-modal matching and then estimate poses. Compared to the best regression-based method Borer~\etal~\cite{borer2024chaos}, our approach achieves a rotation error that is only 0.074$^\circ$ higher and a translation error that is only 0.055\,m higher. Note that we only train on sequence 02, while most direct pose regression methods are typically trained on all KITTI sequences except 00, and some even use KITTI-360~\cite{liao2022kitti} for training. Although direct regression methods achieve strong performance in some metrics, they rely on end-to-end black-box regression without explicit geometric constraints and typically require large amounts of training data. This makes them sensitive to modality gaps, viewpoint changes, and dynamic scenes, leading to limited generalization and interpretability. In contrast, our method first establishes reliable correspondences between LiDAR intensity and RGB images, and then estimates the pose via EPnP. This design not only offers better interpretability and robustness but also achieves significantly better results than all existing matching-based pose estimation methods. Moreover, our performance is competitive with the best direct regression methods in both rotation and translation errors, demonstrating strong overall effectiveness and practical potential. On the other hand, compared to previous matching-based methods, our method significantly outperforms them, reducing the rotation error by 5.385$^\circ$ and the translation error by 0.864\,m. This improvement benefits from the introduction of an enhanced detector-free matching paradigm, which leverages supervised learning to establish robust correspondences between sparse point clouds and images.

In addition, we compare our approach with several matching-based LCEC methods on the KITTI odometry benchmark (sequences 01–08), as shown in Table~\ref{tab.rescmp_kitti_01_08}. Our method achieves consistently better performance, with the lowest average translation error (0.252\,m) and second-lowest rotation error (0.941$^\circ$), demonstrating its robustness and accuracy in diverse driving scenes. Specifically, our method ranks first or second in almost all sequences. Compared to MIAS-LCEC~\cite{zhiwei2024lcec}, which performs well in sequences with richer texture, our method shows more balanced and stable performance across sequences with varying scene structures and viewpoint changes. Traditional methods such as CRLF~\cite{ma2021crlf}, UMich~\cite{pandey2015automatic}, and DVL~\cite{koide2023general} suffer from either large translation or rotation errors due to reliance on sparse features, semantic priors, or flow consistency. Our advantage comes from explicitly learning dense, geometry-aware features across modalities. This allows accurate correspondence supervision without depending on external cues or handcrafted steps, highlighting the effectiveness of our cross-modal matching paradigm for LiDAR-camera calibration. However, our approach shows a relative weakness on sequence 01, which primarily consists of highway scenes where LiDAR points mostly fall on the road surface. This results in fewer distinctive and easily matchable road signs, limiting correspondence quality. Additionally, the performance on sequence 03 is somewhat degraded compared to sequence 02, likely due to a significant data distribution shift between these sequences that affects matching accuracy.


\subsubsection{Dense Point Cloud}
As shown in Table~\ref{tab.lcec_cal}, we compare our approach with several matching-based LCEC methods on the MIAS-LCEC-TF70~\cite{zhiwei2024lcec}. As shown in Table~\ref{tab.lcec_cal}, we compare our approach with several state-of-the-art LCEC methods on the MIAS-LCEC-TF70~\cite{zhiwei2024lcec} benchmark, including CRLF~\cite{ma2021crlf}, UMich~\cite{pandey2015automatic}, HKU-Mars~\cite{yuan2021pixel}, DVL~\cite{koide2023general}, and MIAS-LCEC~\cite{zhiwei2024lcec}. The metrics used are the translation error and rotation error , evaluated across four scene categories: Residential Community, Urban Freeway, Building, and Challenging Weather, as well as the overall performance.

Our method achieves competitive results, with the best rotation error in the Residential Community and Building scenes, and a strong overall rotation performance ($e_r = 0.239$), which is close to the state-of-the-art. However, the translation error in the Urban Freeway and Building scenes is relatively higher compared to other methods. We attribute the lower performance in these two scenes to the limited number of available training samples. Specifically, the Urban Freeway and Building sequences contain only 6 and 7 pairs respectively, resulting in merely 3 pairs available for fine-tuning after dataset splitting. The small amount of training data significantly constrains the network's ability to adapt to these scenes, especially under the domain-specific geometric and environmental variations.



\subsection{Ablation Study}
Table~\ref{tab:ablation} presents the ablation study on the KITTI and nuScenes datasets to evaluate the effectiveness of our key designs. On the KITTI dataset, using a single shared backbone already achieves strong performance, with a high precision of 96.00 and relatively low translation and rotation errors ($e_t = 0.32$, $e_r = 0.81$). This is because the 64-line LiDAR preserves partial texture information, which allows reasonably good feature alignment across modalities even without modality-specific processing.

However, on the nuScenes dataset, the performance of the fine-tuned baseline drops significantly, with a precision of only 85.93 and increased errors ($e_t = 0.91$, $e_r = 3.37$), reflecting the challenges introduced by the much sparser 32-line LiDAR, which lacks sufficient texture cues and leads to a wider modality gap. In this case, introducing a dual backbone becomes crucial. While the benefit of the dual backbone is marginal on KITTI, it contributes more clearly on nuScenes. Though the improvement in error metrics is not dramatic in isolation, this design provides a better foundation for further gains when combined with other components.

The repeatability scoring mechanism plays a particularly vital role on nuScenes. Removing it leads to a noticeable drop in performance: the rotation error rises from 1.73 to 2.03, and the precision decreases from 88.75 to 88.45, indicating that this module helps identify more stable and reliable LiDAR projections across different viewpoints. Its effect is less significant on KITTI, likely due to the already rich structural cues, but still contributes to a slight reduction in rotation error.

The full model, which combines both the dual backbone and repeatability scoring, achieves the best overall performance on nuScenes across all metrics (precision: 88.75, $e_t = 0.65$, $e_r = 1.73$), highlighting the complementary nature of the two modules. The synergy between modality-specific feature extraction and viewpoint-robust keypoint filtering proves to be effective in addressing the challenges posed by sparse and noisy LiDAR data. This comprehensive design ultimately improves the quality of cross-modal correspondences and leads to more accurate and robust registration results.


\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec.conclusion}

In this paper, we extend the classical projection-based framework for point-pixel registration by incorporating attention-based matching and a repeatability-guided prior, enabling robust performance with sparse, single-frame LiDAR data.
By projecting LiDAR intensity and leveraging attention-based dense matching, our approach effectively bridges the modality gap between 3D point clouds and 2D images. To further enhance reliability, we introduce a repeatability-guided score that acts as a soft prior, enabling the suppression of unreliable correspondences caused by occlusion and textureless regions. Extensive experiments on KITTI, nuScenes and MIAS-LCEC-TF70 benchmarks validate the effectiveness of our method. Remarkably, our approach outperforms prior works on nuScenes using only single-frame point clouds, whereas most existing methods rely on temporally accumulated data.  


