\subsubsection{Representing confidence intervals in Praat}

The TextGrid format for speech annotation in Praat currently allows for two different types of tiers: interval tiers and point tiers. An interval tier is made up of a series of intervals that have a defined beginning and end point. Within a given interval tier, intervals cannot overlap. A point tier is made up of a series of instantaneous points that have a single temporal moment of time. Both intervals and points can have a text annotation given to them.

A single interval tier is incapable of containing both confidence intervals and segment labels since the confidence intervals must, by definition, overlap the segment boundaries. Similarly, a single interval tier for just the confidence intervals is inadequate since two adjacent sounds may have their confidence intervals overlap if the duration of one or both is short or if there is a great deal of uncertainty in the estimate. It is, in theory, possible to have a sufficient number of interval tiers that represent alternating intervals. However, it is difficult to determine in advance exactly how many of these alternating tiers would be needed.

What I propose instead is to use point tiers to indicate the low and high edges of the confidence interval. Since points have only an instantaneous temporal extent, they are very unlikely to overlap with each other, save for problems of numerical precision or a highly improbable scenario where the confidence intervals of two separate sounds happen to have the exact same moment as extrema. These points can be labeled to help indicate which sound they are paired with.

\subsection{Results}

The boundary error results from the ensemble alignment are given in Table~\ref{tab:restab}. The values are generally a slight improvement over what \citet{kelleyMasonAlbertaPhoneticSegmenter2024} reported. This improvement is a benefit of using the median of the ensemble since the median is robust to outliers. As a category, forced aligners can have extreme outliers from cascading errors based on maladaptive behaviors of the acoustic models, and the median ameliorates that problem to some degree. Note also that the alignment using dictionary-based transcriptions of words fared worse than using the phonetic transcriptions in the corpora. This was an expected result since speech is such a variable signal, and deviations from dictionary forms are extremely well attested in phonetics \citep{ernestus_introduction_2011, lindblom_explaining_1990, warner_reduced_2019}.

The use of dynamic time warping to assess boundary error also generally shows greater degrees of error. This would be expected using dictionary-based transcriptions since the number of boundaries is not guaranteed to be the same between the system output and the reference alignment. Yet, this pattern is also present when comparing the results of using dynamic time warping to the manual assessment for the manual transcriptions, when, in theory, these values should match. At least in part, this discrepant result has to do with certain annotations in the Buckeye corpus that were ignored during the more manual evaluation that were not ignored in the evaluation with dynamic time warping with minimal pre-processing. These tags indicated events like the interviewer speaking or the speaker laughing. The dynamic time warping technique was intended to be used here with little manipulation of the annotations, so the effect of these labels is not unexpected. More careful evaluation of the labels would allow for this effect to be mitigated, though it does require more time from the user and high familiarity with the data set.

Regarding the size of the confidence intervals, they are somewhat sensitive to outliers in the data since it only takes three poor alignments across the ensemble to include an outlier as part of the confidence interval. This behavior can be seen in the difference between the mean and median width of the confidence intervals presented in Table~\ref{tab:cent}. The median width is noticeably smaller than the frame advance size of 10 ms, suggesting that the ensemble outputs were within one frame of each other more often than not. Regardless, it is important to keep in mind that the confidence interval relates to the uncertainty in the ensemble's output. This uncertainty may not necessarily relate to the quality of the boundary placement itself.

A sample of the alignment is given in Figure~\ref{fig:alignment}. The boundaries in the figure are largely agreeable. However, the dictionary lookup led to some spurious segmentations seen in Figure~\ref{fig:wordseg}. One such example is the [\textipa{\textschwa{}l}] at the end of \textit{...blical}, when it would be more accurate to use [\textipa{\textsyllabic{l}}] if using manual transcription. However, this is a known problem when using standard dictionary lookups.

The confidence intervals in Figure~\ref{fig:segci} can be hard to see since they are narrow, though the pattern is in line with the data in Table~\ref{tab:cent}. It is worth remarking that boundaries that are intuitively harder, such as between vowels and approximants as in [\textipa{Al}], have wider confidence intervals than easier boundaries, such as between vowels and fricatives as in [\textipa{\textrhookschwa{}z}]. Regarding the graphical display, the labels in the point tier are difficult to separate from each other. Although, in an object-oriented programming sense, the overlapping is an inherent shortcoming of using only the built-in objects in Praat to approximate a boundary type object that can sensibly display both boundaries and labels.

