The partial trace is used to obtain the reduced density matrix of a subsystem from the density matrix of a larger composite system. Consider a composite quantum system that consists of two subsystems, \(A\) and \(B\). The state of the entire system is described by a density matrix \(\rho\) on the combined Hilbert space \(\mathcal{H}_A \otimes \mathcal{H}_B\). The partial trace over subsystem \(B\), denoted by \(\text{Tr}_B\), is an operation that traces out the degrees of freedom associated with subsystem \(B\), resulting in the reduced density matrix \(\rho_A\) for subsystem \(A\). Let \(\{|i\rangle_B\}\) be an orthonormal basis for the Hilbert space \(\mathcal{H}_B\). The partial trace over \(B\) is defined by
$\rho_A = \text{Tr}_B (\rho) = \sum_i \langle i|_B \rho |i\rangle_B.
$
In other words, \(\rho_A\) is obtained by summing over the diagonal elements of the density matrix \(\rho\) for the basis states of subsystem \(B\). This process effectively "traces out" subsystem \(B\), leaving a density matrix that describes the statistical properties and correlations of subsystem \(A\) alone.
