         \textbf{Black-cloud Effect (BCE) } & This signifies an excessive amount of information to process because organized communication is deficient, limited opportunities for knowledge sharing (such as collaborations, discussions, and daily stand-up meetings), and a shortage of experienced project members who can bridge the community's knowledge and experience gap \cite{tamburri2015social, 8651329}. \\ \midrule
         \textbf{Prima-donnas Effect (PDE) } & This issue arises when a group of individuals is resistant to accepting external changes suggested by fellow team members because of poorly organized collaboration within the community \cite{10.1109/52.819967, tamburri2015social, 8651329}. \\ \midrule
         \textit{Sharing Valley (SV)} & This problem arises due to the absence of effective information exchange activities, such as face-to-face meetings. The primary consequence of this limitation is that community members exchange crucial knowledge that may be outdated, incorrect, or unverified \cite{tamburri2015social}. \\
         \midrule
         \textbf{Organizational Skirmish (OS) } & The origin of the Operating Smell (OS) lies in the mismatch between various levels of expertise and communication channels among development units or individuals participating in the project. This issue frequently results in reduced productivity and has an impact on the project's schedule and budget \cite{tamburri2015social}. \\ \midrule
         \textbf{Solution Defiance (SD) } & The Smell of Solution Defiance (SD) emerges when there are varying cultural and experiential backgrounds within the development community. These differences result in the community splitting into distinct subgroups with entirely contradictory views on technical or socio-technical choices. The presence of SD frequently causes unforeseen project delays and fosters uncooperative behaviour among the developers \cite{tamburri2015social}. \\ \midrule
         \textbf{Radio Silence (RS) }  & The Radio Silence smell arises when there is an excessive formality in regular procedures, stemming from the ineffective structural organization within a community. This RS community smell commonly leads to the slowing down of changes and the wastage of valuable time as a result of intricate and inflexible formal procedures. The primary consequence of this smell is an unanticipated significant delay in the decision-making process due to the obligatory formal actions \cite{tamburri2015social}. \\ \midrule
         \textbf{Truck Factor Smell (TFS) } & The Truck Factor Smell (TFS) emerges when the majority of project information and knowledge is centralized in one or a few developers. This smell's existence ultimately results in a substantial loss of knowledge when developers leave the project \cite{avelino2016novel}.\\ \midrule
         \textbf{Unhealthy Interaction (UI) } & This smell arises when developer discussions are sluggish, superficial, concise, or of low quality. It is characterized by limited developer engagement in project discussions (e.g., pull requests, issues, etc.) and extended delays between message exchanges \cite{9397528}. \\ \midrule
        \textbf{Toxic Communication (TC) } & This smell is present when developer communications involve harmful dialogues and express negative emotions, including dissatisfaction, anger, or discordant viewpoints on different issues under discussion. Developers may engage in negative social interactions with their colleagues, potentially causing frustration and stress. These adverse interactions can ultimately lead to developers disengaging from projects \cite{9397528, 10.1007/s10664-017-9526-0}.\\
        \bottomrule
    \end{tabular}
    }

    \label{tab:community-smell-details}
\end{table*}
In particular, this study answered \textit{four} research questions and made \textit{four} key contributions, as outlined below:

\textbf{RQ1: To what extent is the prevalence of community smell in ML-based systems?} \\
We examine the prevalence of various community smells in machine learning (ML) projects to understand how socio-technical issues manifest in these systems. This analysis is essential for identifying early collaboration bottlenecks, guiding organizational improvements, and fostering healthier ML development practices.

\textbf{RQ2: To what extent do community smells co-occur with SATD in ML-based systems?}\\
We investigate the relationship between community smells and the presence of SATD in ML projects. Understanding this co-occurrence helps uncover how socio-technical issues contribute to technical debt accumulation and informs targeted strategies for mitigating both.

\textbf{RQ3: What types of SATD are most associated with specific community smells?}\\
We analyze the associations between individual community smells and distinct types of SATD to identify which socio-technical issues most strongly contribute to specific forms of technical debt. This insight supports more focused interventions to address the root causes of recurring debt patterns in ML-based systems.

\textbf{RQ4: How do community smells and SATD evolve over releases in ML projects of different sizes?} 
We examine the temporal trends of community smells and SATD across small, medium, and large ML projects to understand how these issues progress throughout a project’s lifecycle. This analysis highlights size-specific patterns and informs proactive debt management and collaboration strategies over time.

