\section{Threats to Validity}
Threats to \textbf{construct validity} involve the alignment between theory and observation. Our study relies on the detection capabilities of automated tools, csDetector for community smells, and MT-BERT-SATD for identifying SATD. While both tools have demonstrated strong performance in prior work, they are inherently limited by their trained heuristics and model architectures. csDetector may miss smells occurring in off-platform or informal communication, and the SATD classifier may mislabel comments that do not follow conventional SATD phrasing. To address this, we conducted robustness checks and manual validation on a subset of projects to ensure the reliability of our findings. Nonetheless, we acknowledge that not all community smells or SATD instances may be captured, and our results reflect patterns detectable by the tools. To maintain clarity, we explicitly refer to our findings as ``community smells detectable by csDetector,” recognizing the tool's scope and limitations.


Threats to \textbf{internal validity} concern factors internal to our study that can influence the results. To align the detection windows of community smells and SATD, we performed release-based analysis. This assumes that the technical and socio-technical issues emerge and interact within the same release cycle. While this offers a practical and structured view of their co-evolution, there may be latent lags in how community smells manifest into SATD or vice versa. Additionally, due to the stochastic nature of the ML model used in csDetector, small variations in smell detection could occur across runs. We mitigated this by performing multiple runs and validating the results manually in a subset of projects.


Threats to \textbf{external validity} concern the generalizability of our findings. Our dataset, drawn from 155 active, high-quality ML projects curated by Latendresse et al.\cite{latendresse2024exploratory}, strengthens the reliability of our results but may not fully represent immature, experimental, or closed-source projects. Additionally, as the projects are primarily from GitHub, they may not capture collaboration practices common in corporate environments.

Threats to \textbf{conclusion validity} relate to whether the conclusions drawn from the analysis are statistically and logically sound.
We used Spearman’s rank and Point-Biserial correlation to analyze the relationship between community smells and SATD. While these tests are appropriate for the data types and distributions involved, correlation does not imply causation. The observed associations highlight co-occurrence patterns but do not definitively establish that community smells cause SATD or vice versa. Our trend analysis shows temporal similarities but does not confirm directional dependencies. Future work should explore causal inference models to strengthen these findings.

\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
In this study, we conducted an empirical investigation into the relationship between community smells and SATD in 155 open-source ML-based software projects. Our analysis revealed that community smells are not only prevalent in ML systems but also closely associated with the accumulation and persistence of SATD. We identified specific smells, such as Radio Silence, Organizational Silo, and Sharing Villainy, as strong indicators of technical debt, particularly in requirements, design, and documentation. Furthermore, our release-level trend analysis shows that both community smells and SATD tend to escalate as projects scale, with large projects exhibiting high variability and persistence across most debt categories. These findings highlight the importance of tracking socio-technical indicators over time and emphasize the value of targeted interventions at major release points.

For future work, we plan to conduct qualitative studies involving developer interviews to better understand the contextual and cultural factors behind community smells. We also aim to explore the impact of community smell resolution on subsequent SATD reduction. Additionally, we hope to enhance detection accuracy by incorporating more advanced large language models and extending our analysis to industrial and non-GitHub ML projects, offering broader insights into socio-technical debt across diverse development environments.

