We can now prove the main result of this section in the special case that $C$ is a strictly convex body. We generalize \Cref{thm:strict} to arbitrary convex bodies in \Cref{thm:body} at the end of \Cref{ssec_key}.
If $C$ is strictly convex, every tangent line of $C$ has a unique intersection point with $C$. Consequently, the parallel tangent lines $L_i$ and $L_i'$ uniquely determine $p_i$ and $q_i$ for $i\in \{1,2,3\}$. Furthermore, the points $p_i$ and $q_i$ continuously depend on (the direction of) the line $L_i$.  




